From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Date: Sat, 3 Oct 2026 17:12:25 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] feat(allwinner): enable the A733 CPU_BACK_PLL at cold boot

The kernel's CPU PLL controller relocks a cluster PLL by moving
the cluster's clock mux off it and back. UM V1.00 5.1.4.1 "PLL Distribution"
(printed p.243 / PDF p.264) gives every core and the cluster three backup
sources: 26 MHz, CPU_BACK_PLL and a fixed peripheral rate. Today the park
lands on the 26 MHz DCXO, because CPU_BACK_PLL is off: its control
register reads 0x40104A00 (PLL_EN 0) -- boot0, BL31 and U-Boot all
leave it at reset. The kernel can park on CPU_BACK_PLL when it is
enabled and falls back to the DCXO when it is not. This patch enables it.

WHERE, AND WHY BL31. bl31_platform_setup() runs once per cold boot on the
boot core, after boot0 has programmed the cluster PLLs and before BL33, so
before anything in the normal world can touch a CPU clock. It is also where
the other SoC-specific clock setup in this TF-A already lives (the A64/H5 AHB
tweaks in the same function), and EL3 is the owner of CPU power here (PSCI,
the CPU ops). U-Boot proper has no CPU PLL code for this SoC at all: the only
CPU_PLL_CFG writer in the tree is SPL's clock_a523_set_cpu_plls(), and boot0
replaces SPL in this chain, so there is no "platform clock init" there to
extend. A resident firmware stage also covers any BL33, not just ours. The
call sits after generic_delay_timer_init(), which the lock poll needs.

OPEN: WHERE THE ENABLE BELONGS. The only consumer of this PLL is the
kernel's CPU clock driver, which parks the clusters on it during a
relock. The clean place for the enable is that driver: it can prepare
and enable its parking source through the clock framework before it
reparents, and then nothing in the kernel depends on what a firmware
stage did before it, whatever the BL33. That kernel change does not
exist yet; until it does, the firmware keeps the enable described here,
and this patch is to be dropped together with it.

WHAT IS WRITTEN. CPU_PLL_CFG base 0x08870000 (UM V1.00 module list, printed
p.249 / PDF p.270), CPU_BACK_PLL_CTRL_REG +0x0000 (UM V1.00 5.1.10.1, printed
p.284-285 / PDF p.305-306): PLL_EN 31, PLL_LDO_EN 30, LOCK_ENABLE 29, LOCK
28 (RO), PLL_OUTPUT_GATE 27, PLL_OUTPUT_DIV 22:20 (P0 = field + 1), PLL_N
15:8 (N = field + 1), PLL_INPUT_DIV 1 (M1 = field + 1), rate =
InputFreq * N / M1 / P0. Only the four enable bits are written. The factors
stay at reset -- N 75, M1 1, P0 2, i.e. 26 MHz * 75 / 2 = 975 MHz on our
26 MHz DCXO (the UM's default of 900 MHz is the same formula at 24 MHz),
measured on silicon at 974.6 MHz (2026-09-23). If an earlier stage
ever changes a factor, the PLL is left off rather than enabled at a rate
nobody measured.

SEQUENCE. UM V1.00 5.1.4.3, the first of its two PLL enable methods, the
one the UM names for the startup phase (printed p.246 / PDF p.267), in
short:

  1. PLL_LDO_EN = 1.
  2. PLL_OUTPUT_GATE = 1.
  3. Program the factors N, M1, M0 and P.
  4. PLL_EN = 1.
  5. LOCK_ENABLE = 1.
  6. Poll LOCK until it reads 1; the UM asks for that judgement three times.
  7. Wait 20 us before anything uses the PLL.

Step 3 writes nothing (reset factors). The three reads of step 6 follow the
UM's rule for judging PLL lock (UM V1.00 4.1.4.2, printed p.27 / PDF p.48):
LOCK is read 3 us apart, and three reads of 1 count as stable -- so LOCK is
sampled every 3 us and must read 1 on three consecutive samples. Step 1 also clears
LOCK_ENABLE, so step 5 is always a 0 -> 1 edge: LOCK is not cleared by
PLL_EN = 0 (a run-time disable read back 0x50104A00, 2026-09-23). No
cluster mux selects this PLL yet, so Method 1's glitch at gate-before-enable
reaches nothing.

Guards: runs only if PLL_EN reads 0 (idempotent; an already-running PLL is
reported and left alone); the lock poll is bounded (20000 samples, >= 60 ms).
On timeout the PLL is taken down in the UM's "Disable PLLs" order (gate,
LOCK_ENABLE, PLL_EN), PLL_LDO_EN is restored to what it read before, one
NOTICE line says so, and the boot continues; the kernel then parks on the
DCXO as it does today. Every outcome prints one NOTICE line with the
register before and after:

  NOTICE:  BL31: CPU_BACK_PLL 0x40104a00 -> 0xf8104a00, enabled at reset factors

(expected value: the vendor kernel's run-time enable reads 0xF8104A00.)

WHERE THE VENDOR DRIVER DIFFERS. Read for reference only, not ported:
radxa/allwinner-bsp drivers/clk/sunxi-ng/ccu-sun60iw2-cpupll.c. The boot
firmware never enables the PLL; the vendor kernel does it at probe time,
and differs from the UM and from this patch in these ways:
- Rate: pll_cpu_back_clk is a ccu_nm with N at offset 0 and a 4-bit M at
  bits 3:0, and no P0. The UM has N = field + 1, a 1-bit M1 at bit 1 (bits
  0 and 4:2 reserved) and P0 at 22:20. Its clk_summary therefore reports
  26 MHz * 74 = 1924 MHz for a PLL running at 975 -- off by 2x in the one
  number a parking policy needs.
- Sequence: PLL_EN, LDO_EN, LOCK_ENABLE and OUTPUT_GATE go in one write,
  which is neither of the UM's two methods, followed by a single LOCK poll
  (no three-read judgement, no 20 us). Before that it writes 1 to bit 26 and
  waits for it to clear -- the linear PLLs' PLL_UPDATE, reserved in
  CPU_BACK_PLL_CTRL_REG.
- It also sets PAT0 bits 29/30 and PAT1 bit 31 on the back PLL (its
  comments: "Triangular(3bit)" pattern, "lfm_en") with no step of either
  enable method asking for it; we leave both pattern registers at reset.
- Timeouts are WARN_ON and carry on with the PLL half-configured.
- Its VCO-range table for a 26 MHz DCXO assigns n.min twice (the second was
  meant to be n.max); moot for us, we never write N.
What we do instead: enable in the firmware, once, by the UM's startup-phase
method, at the reset rate, with a bounded, judged lock and a clean give-up.

Assisted-by: Claude
